{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed807260302/17/codegen_prompts/3.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed807260302/17`\n- 题号：`3`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed807260302/17/3.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"3\">\n- `description`：读取网格边缘的彩色路标序列，将内部迷宫路径按路标顺序逐段重新着色。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为黑色。\n  - 网格内部有一条由灰色像素构成的连续单像素宽路径（四连通），路径没有分支，从一个端点延伸到另一个端点。路径的两个端点分别位于网格的不同边缘行/列上。\n  - 网格的最上方一行（第 0 行）包含若干个彩色像素（非黑色、非灰色），它们之间以黑色间隔，从左到右排列形成一个有序的颜色序列，例如：红、蓝、绿、黄。这些颜色像素不与路径相连。\n  - 路径上散布着若干个品红色标记像素，将路径分割成若干段。品红色标记像素也属于路径的一部分。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步：识别路径方向。找到路径的两个端点。端点 A 定义为距离第 0 行颜色序列最左侧像素更近（行+列之和更小）的那个端点，端点 B 为另一个端点。路径方向为从 A 到 B。\n  - 第二步：分段。沿路径从 A 走到 B，每遇到一个品红色标记像素就结束当前段并开始新一段（品红色像素本身归入前一段的末尾）。设共有 K 段。\n  - 第三步：着色。读取第 0 行从左到右的颜色序列，共 K 个颜色。将路径的第 i 段全部重新着色为序列中第 i 个颜色（i 从 1 开始），包括该段末尾的品红色标记像素。\n  - 第 0 行的颜色序列像素在输出中保持不变。\n\n- `Key insight`：关键在于发现三个独立的非显然对应关系——（1）第 0 行像素是颜色调色板而非装饰；（2）品红色像素是路径分段符而非普通路径色；（3）路径的遍历方向由端点与调色板的空间接近性决定。三层推理叠加使得规则不易从单个样例猜出。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：train/f8f52ecc, eval/dbff022c, train/afe3afe9, train/f1bcbc2c, train/538b439f, train/5623160b, train/e4941b18, eval/581f7754\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f8f52ecc\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3623\nmechanism=0.4591\nlexical=0.0105\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:path, object:sprite, operation:bfs_distance, operation:recolor, operation:sort_order, output:same_size, relation:between, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/f8f52ecc.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/f8f52ecc.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nConnect same-colored foreground components with one-cell-wide orthogonal paths around teal wall segments.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Teal cells are fixed wall segments and are never source connectors.\n- Other foreground colors appear as one or more 4-connected components; colors with a single component stay unchanged.\n\nRule\n- For each non-background, non-teal color, read its 4-connected components in row-major order.\n- Connect each consecutive pair by the shortest clear L-shaped path between the two components.\n- Clear paths may pass only through background or the same color, and they preserve the previous travel axis when both bends are possible.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is not a global recolor: each color traces a routed chain through its own components while teal cells and other colors act as blockers.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"non-background, non-teal colors with multiple 4-connected components\",\n  \"path\": \"from each component to the next same-colored component in row-major order, take a shortest clear one-bend horizontal/vertical path through background or same color; teal wall segments and other colors block the path\",\n  \"dst\": \"next same-colored component in the ordered chain\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"paint every background pixel on each connector path with the source color\",\n  \"transform\": \"keep teal wall segments, single-component colors, and all original foreground pixels unchanged\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"process each color's components in row-major order; when both bends are clear, preserve the previous travel axis, otherwise use the longer-axis-first clear bend\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dbff022c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3176\nmechanism=0.3581\nlexical=0.0106\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:grid_edge, object:path, object:sprite, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dbff022c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/dbff022c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.\n- A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.\n\nRule:\n- Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.\n- Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.\n- For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.\n\nKey insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Use a color palette legend on the grid edge to fill sprites' interiors based on their border color mapping.\",\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"hollow sprites with colored borders s\",\n  \"dst\": \"interior of same sprite d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"d.fill_color == palette_lookup(s.border_color)\",\n  \"path\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill d with palette[s.border_color]\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"palette is 2xN or Nx2 block on grid edge; read direction based on edge position: top edge reads top-to-bottom, bottom edge reads bottom-to-top, left edge reads left-to-right; first row/column is border color, second is fill color\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: locate 2xN or Nx2 palette legend on grid edge; Step 2: build color mapping (border -> fill) based on reading direction; Step 3: for each hollow sprite, lookup border color in palette and fill interior with corresponding fill color\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"color-mapping\",\n    \"lookup-table\",\n    \"flood-fill\",\n    \"border-detection\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"The challenge is correctly reading the palette legend based on its edge position and orientation\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"afe3afe9\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3263\nmechanism=0.4061\nlexical=0.0106\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:path, object:sprite, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/afe3afe9.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/afe3afe9.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nConvert each hollow 3x3 symbol to one pixel and slide the pixels clockwise from the marked edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a background, one full-edge marker line, and monochrome hollow 3x3 foreground symbols.\n- The symbols lie on a coarse row/column lattice; fully empty lattice rows or columns are absent from the output.\n\nRule\n- Replace each hollow 3x3 symbol with one pixel of its color in the corresponding coarse-grid cell.\n- The marker edge gives the compaction direction by clockwise border travel: top -> right, right -> down, bottom -> left, left -> up.\n- Preserve the order of non-background pixels while sliding them as far as possible in that direction.\n\nKey insight\n- The large picture is a scaled symbolic grid, and the blue edge marker acts as a gravity direction rather than as output content.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"hollow 3x3 foreground symbols collapsed to one coarse-grid pixel each; the full-edge blue marker selects the path direction\",\n  \"path\": \"straight along each coarse row or column in clockwise border direction from the marker edge: top->right, right->down, bottom->left, left->up; stop when packed against the boundary or previous pixel\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"stably compact all non-background coarse pixels in each parallel line, preserving their order\",\n  \"transform\": \"discard the blue marker and fully empty coarse rows or columns; replace each hollow 3x3 symbol by its color\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"occupied coarse lattice after empty rows or columns are dropped\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f1bcbc2c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3156\nmechanism=0.3727\nlexical=0.0109\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:grid_edge, object:path, object:sprite, operation:recolor, output:same_size, relation:between\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/f1bcbc2c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/f1bcbc2c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nPaint the top-connected middle channel between two orange path components teal.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is black.\n- One foreground color forms exactly two 4-connected one-pixel-wide path components.\n- Optional non-background control pixels may sit in the channel between the two paths.\n\nRule\n- Dilate each path component by one cell in the 8-neighborhood and intersect the two dilations; this gives the one-cell-wide middle channel.\n- A control pixel in that channel is a blocker only when its 4-neighbors touch fewer than both path components; otherwise it is part of the channel and is repainted.\n- After removing blockers, paint the channel component connected to the top edge teal and keep all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The desired pixels are not found by region filling; they are the geometric overlap of the two paths' one-cell halos, with weakly attached control pixels splitting the channel.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"middle-channel mask formed by intersecting the 8-neighborhood halos of the two orange 4-connected path objects\",\n  \"transform\": \"recolor the top-connected passable channel to teal; a non-bg control pixel blocks the channel only if it is not 4-adjacent to both orange path objects, otherwise it is recolored too\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"538b439f\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3113\nmechanism=0.3486\nlexical=0.0110\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:path, object:sprite, operation:recolor, output:same_size, relation:between, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/538b439f.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/538b439f.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nReflect the solid rectangular source objects across the full separator line and color the gap with the separator color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background contains one full-width or full-height one-pixel\n  separator line in a non-background color.\n- One other foreground color forms solid rectangular objects, all strictly on the same side of that separator.\n- Remaining non-background pixels are scattered distractors.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input grid shape and unchanged pixels outside each affected corridor.\n- Mirror each source object across the separator line.\n- For every source object, recolor the rectangular strip between the\n  original object and its reflection to the separator color, then paint the\n  reflected object in the source color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The full straight line is a mirror axis; the real objects are identified\n  structurally as the only solid rectangles confined to one side, while\n  scattered pixels are noise that may be overwritten inside the corridor.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"solid rectangular foreground objects all on one side of the full separator row/column\",\n  \"dst\": \"their mirror positions across the separator line\",\n  \"transform\": \"copy each source object by reflection; recolor the rectangular strip between each source object and its mirror to the separator color, overwriting background or distractor pixels inside that strip\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5623160b\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3220\nmechanism=0.3995\nlexical=0.0113\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:path, object:sprite, operation:bfs_distance, output:same_size, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/5623160b.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/5623160b.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nPush every colored object touching maroon markers straight outward until it reaches the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
